# This node is used as a balancing node for excess refinery gas. It is mostly needed because some
# countries use a lot of refinery gas in CHPs, this flow isn't modelled explicitly. There are also
# some accounting type flows on the energy balance. For many countries, the supply is therefore
# higher than the demand for refinery gas in the industry sector. This is a dead end in the graph.
# For NL, however, the demand for refinery gas in industry is high and this node is 0.
#
# In the present year it receives the refinery gas production that exceeds the industry demand
# (the demand of industry_final_demand_refinery_gas). In future years it receives a constant
# share of the locally available refinery gas, so it scales with refinery production.
#
# The demand is set here because the present-year balance has two unknowns that close the same
# gap: the crude oil top-up and the excess to this node. Without this formula Refinery cannot
# choose between them (IncalculableGraphError). A flexible/inversed_flexible setup like
# import/export does not work here either: Refinery cannot solve it, and with a converter node
# ETEngine never uses the top-up because nothing downstream pulls demand.

- use = energetic
- groups = []
- graph_methods = [demand]
- free_co2_factor = 0.0

~ demand =
    [
      EB(oil_refineries_transformation, refinery_gas) - (
        EB(industry, refinery_gas) -
        EB(energy_industry_own_use, refinery_gas) +
        EB("own_use_in_electricity,_CHP_and_heat_plants", refinery_gas)
      ),
      0.0
    ].max
